WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:06.200
"Cognitive Stimulation, Music Therapy, and
Cryostimulation to Improve Cognitive Function"

00:00:07.114 --> 00:00:08.820
In my book, How Not to Age,

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:11.520
I discuss the role
of drugs and supplements

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:13.500
and focus on diet
and lifestyle approaches

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:16.620
to the prevention and treatment
of cognitive decline,

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:20.220
but there are a few common
non-drug, non-supplement,

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:22.680
non-lifestyle approaches
to dementia treatment.

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:25.620
For example,
cognitive stimulation therapy,

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:27.300
which typically involves
group activities

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:28.320
and social interaction.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:32.040
There have been at least 44
randomized controlled trials

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:33.180
of cognitive stimulation

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:35.040
including thousands
of participants.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:39.780
ADAS-Cog, short for Alzheimer's
Disease Assessment Scale

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:40.860
Cognitive subscale,

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:43.140
is the most commonly used
measure of cognition

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:44.820
in clinical trials
of dementia treatment.

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:46.860
A clinically relevant
improvement

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:49.800
is often defined as a reduction
of four or more points

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:51.360
on the ADAS-Cog measure.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:54.600
Regrettably, the attenuation
of cognitive decline

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:57.000
for most of the cognitive
stimulation studies

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:58.740
averaged only about two points.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:01.500
Now you could argue,
look, any improvement

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:02.520
is better than nothing,

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:04.740
but unfortunately,
the cognitive effects

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:06.120
don't appear to generalize

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:08.400
to helping
with everyday life situations.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.140
You can train people
to get better

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:12.120
at a computerized "brain game,"

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:15.780
but that doesn't necessarily
translate to other arenas.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:19.020
Also, the marginal improvements
are temporary.

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:20.400
They don't seem to persist

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.020
once the intervention period
is over.

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:24.780
While cognitive
stimulation therapy

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:27.180
may have no significant
overall effect

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:28.500
on quality of life,

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:31.260
any kind of group therapy
has been shown

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:33.360
to improve
psychological well-being.

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:34.980
As social creatures,

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:37.440
social participation is
important for mental health

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:38.700
throughout the life course,

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:40.740
but there's
insufficient evidence

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:43.680
that it can improve or prevent
cognitive dysfunction.

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:47.160
Dementia is associated
with social isolation,

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:49.560
but this may be
reverse causation,

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:51.900
where the dementia leads
to social withdrawal,

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:53.220
instead of the other way around.

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:56.640
The same issue
comes up all the time

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:57.540
with those advocating

00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:00.060
for "use-it-or-lose-it"
mental stimulation

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:01.800
to forestall decline.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:03.240
For example, this review

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:05.640
in a peer-reviewed
aging journal suggested

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:08.100
that "if a person
solves crosswords

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:09.360
four times a week,

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:12.060
they can reduce their risk
of dementia by 47 percent."

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:15.630
Yes, doing
frequent crossword puzzles

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:18.900
is associated with lower
subsequent dementia risk,

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:20.100
but isn't it more likely

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:22.680
that those on the verge
of a dementia diagnosis

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:25.020
are less likely to be doing
crossword puzzles,

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:27.600
as a result of their declining
cognitive abilities?

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:31.020
Music therapy presents
a similar story

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:32.400
of improving well-being

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:34.500
without necessarily
affecting cognition.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:37.200
Even in late stages
of dementia,

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:38.880
when verbal communication
is lost,

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:41.340
music can be enjoyed.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:44.340
However, it does not seem
to improve brain function.

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:46.620
Most (five out of seven)
meta-analyses

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:49.020
of the 40+ interventional
clinical trials

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:50.820
of music therapy
for people with dementia

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:52.980
found no cognitive benefits.

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:54.660
And the few studies that did

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:56.100
found the effect
was short-lived,

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:57.960
disappearing
after one to three months.

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:01.140
Even though cognition
doesn't seem to be affected,

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:02.880
there still may be
positive effects

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:04.320
in terms of reducing agitation

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:06.660
and improving mood
and socialization.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:11.940
One of the kookiest treatments
I ran across was cryotherapy,

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:13.800
also known as cryostimulation.

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:16.260
Those with mild
cognitive impairment

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:18.000
randomized
to short-term exposures

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.020
to extremely cold temperatures
experienced a transient boost

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:24.180
in general
cognitive functioning

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:27.780
(no longer apparent two weeks
after daily sessions ended).

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:30.360
Study subjects in shorts
and a T-shirt

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:33.780
were exposed to 58 degrees
below zero (-50 C)

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:34.380
for two minutes.

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:39.300
No, wait, that was
the placebo control group

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:41.940
exposed to "noncryogenic"
temperatures.

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:44.940
How low
did the experimental group go?

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:46.140
Two minutes

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:56.471
at more than 200 degrees
below zero (-130 C).

